const QB={
eng:[
{id:"V1",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your product manager sends a ticket: 'Improve user retention.' The codebase and data are available. Before engaging AI you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Start building immediately - the requirement is clear enough to begin.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Clarify with the PM which user segment or metric defines retention.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Write a one-paragraph problem statement defining what retention means in this product context, which technical intervention is most likely to move it, and what success looks like - before writing a single prompt.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the PM to provide a full technical specification before you engage.",s:1}]},
{id:"V2",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"AI produces a technically correct fix for the bug you reported. Reviewing it, you notice the fix addresses the symptom but not the underlying architectural issue that caused it. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Ship the fix - it resolves the immediate issue and passes tests.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Add a code comment flagging the deeper architectural concern.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Refactor around the root cause rather than the symptom - a symptom fix will reproduce the same bug in a different form within weeks.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the architectural concern in the next sprint and ship the symptom fix now.",s:1}]},
{id:"V3",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"You are three hours into an AI-assisted code refactor when you realize you are solving for performance when the actual bottleneck is network latency - a different problem entirely. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Complete the refactor - it will still improve performance even if it is not the primary bottleneck.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Complete the refactor but flag the latency issue in the PR description.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Stop and restart around the actual bottleneck - a well-executed solution to the wrong problem costs more in the long run than the time lost restarting.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ship both approaches and let the team benchmark which produces better results.",s:1}]},
{id:"V4",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A junior engineer asks you to review their AI-assisted solution before merging. The code is sound but solves a constraint that no longer exists - the requirement changed two sprints ago. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Approve the PR - the code is correct and the ticket was the source of truth.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Leave a comment pointing out the discrepancy and let them decide.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Sit with them, trace back to what the system actually needs now, and redesign the solution together - treating this as a systems thinking development moment.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reassign the ticket to the PM to clarify before any code is merged.",s:1}]},
{id:"V5",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your engineering org is about to invest six months of AI-assisted development in a platform built on an architectural decision made eighteen months ago. You believe that decision was made for constraints that no longer exist and the resulting system will be expensive to maintain and difficult to scale. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Build what was scoped - the architectural decision was agreed and reopening it creates conflict.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise your concern informally with the tech lead.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Formally document the architectural concern with a cost-benefit analysis and present it to engineering leadership - accepting the friction this creates and taking accountability for the delay.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Build the scoped solution but design it with an abstraction layer that makes future re-architecture cheaper.",s:1}]},
{id:"E1",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are demoing an AI-assisted feature to the CTO in 45 minutes. The output is functionally correct but one edge case produces unexpected behavior - you cannot immediately identify why. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Demo as planned - the core functionality works and the edge case is unlikely to come up.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Remove the edge case from the demo flow without flagging it.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Spend the 45 minutes isolating the unexpected behavior - then either fix it or demo with an explicit callout: 'This edge case produces unexpected behavior I have not yet diagnosed.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Postpone the demo until you have fully diagnosed the behavior.",s:1}]},
{id:"E2",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"Your team has followed an AI-recommended microservices architecture for three weeks. Performance metrics are below expectations. The AI model continues to recommend scaling the current approach with high confidence. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue scaling - three weeks is not enough to override a high-confidence architectural recommendation.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise the concern with your tech lead and defer to their call.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call to re-evaluate the architecture based on your pattern recognition - document your technical reasoning and take accountability for the direction change.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Commission a performance audit with different benchmarking parameters before deciding.",s:1}]},
{id:"E3",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are leading an AI integration project and the model output contradicts what your engineering director expects based on prior system behavior. The AI confidence is high. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the AI output as-is - high confidence is high confidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust the output presentation to reduce the contradiction with prior expectations.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the finding clearly, explain your technical assessment of why it contradicts prior behavior, and engage the engineering director with the tension directly.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Rerun the model with different hyperparameters until the output aligns with expected behavior.",s:1}]},
{id:"E4",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are the most senior engineer available to make a critical production decision. You have 60% of the diagnostic information you would want. Every hour of delay costs the business significantly. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Wait for the remaining diagnostic data - production decisions require adequate information.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Escalate to engineering leadership so someone more senior takes accountability.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call with 60% information, document your reasoning and assumptions, name the signals that would cause you to reverse it, and take full accountability.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Make a provisional fix and communicate it explicitly as temporary pending full diagnosis.",s:1}]},
{id:"E5",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"An AI-assisted code review tool your team has used for two months has been systematically missing a class of security vulnerability. Some approved PRs have already been merged to production. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Quietly patch the vulnerability class and audit merged PRs internally before informing anyone.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Inform your tech lead and let engineering leadership decide how to handle disclosure.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Immediately disclose the vulnerability class to all relevant stakeholders - security, product, and leadership - even before the full audit is complete, accepting the reputational cost in exchange for avoiding the trust cost of delayed disclosure.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Complete the full security audit first, then disclose only to stakeholders whose code was actually affected.",s:1}]},
{id:"C1",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are running a technical design review. The engineering director agrees with every architectural proposal quickly. The junior engineers stay largely silent. As the review ends you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Feel satisfied - director endorsement is the most important signal in a design review.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Note the dynamic and plan to follow up with the junior engineers in a separate forum.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize that rapid senior agreement combined with junior silence often signals unspoken technical concern - and before the review closes, create an explicit moment for junior engineers to raise issues or alternative approaches.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the engineering director directly whether the team fully agrees.",s:1}]},
{id:"C2",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"In a sprint retrospective, two senior engineers disagree sharply about technical approach. One concedes within minutes and the retro moves on. Afterward you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the agreed approach - the disagreement was resolved.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in with the engineer who conceded to make sure they are comfortable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Note that the speed of concession is unlikely to reflect genuine technical alignment - and find a private moment to surface whether the unresolved disagreement will affect code quality before the next sprint.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the dynamic with the engineering manager so they are aware.",s:1}]},
{id:"C3",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"A developer who has been one of your most engaged reviewers has become progressively quieter in code reviews over the past three weeks. They still ship on time but their review comments have become minimal. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Note no concern - they are still delivering.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in briefly to ask if they are okay.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize the behavioral shift as a meaningful signal - find a low-pressure private moment to genuinely understand what has changed, without presupposing what you will hear.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Flag the change in engagement to the engineering manager as a potential team health risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"C4",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are in an architectural decision meeting. The technical data clearly supports a cloud-native approach. The VP of Engineering is signaling through tone and body language - not words - that they are uncomfortable with the cloud dependency. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the technical recommendation regardless of the signal - the data should drive the decision.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Soften your recommendation to leave room for the VP's implicit preference.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the technical recommendation clearly, then address the tension directly: 'I notice there may be a concern about the cloud dependency I haven't fully addressed - I'd like to understand it before we finalize.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Speak with the VP privately before the meeting to understand their concern and adjust the recommendation accordingly.",s:1}]},
{id:"C5",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You have been embedded with a client engineering team to assess their AI readiness. The CTO's narrative is positive and committed. After two days working alongside mid-level engineers, you sense significant technical debt anxiety and passive resistance to the AI tooling beneath the official narrative. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Report on the CTO's official narrative - your brief was to assess AI readiness and leadership has confirmed commitment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Include a brief note that 'some concerns about tooling adoption were observed.'",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present a candid technical assessment that distinguishes the official narrative from the engineering reality - naming the debt anxiety and skill concerns with evidence from your two days on the ground.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Share your observations with the CTO verbally before writing the report to understand how much candor they want in the document.",s:1}]},
{id:"O1",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you approach AI coding assistance on significant engineering problems:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I paste the problem or error into AI and work with what comes back. I refine if the first output is not useful.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a rough sense of what I need before prompting, but I rarely write this down or structure it explicitly.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before engaging AI on a significant engineering problem, I write a clear problem definition - the constraint, the expected behavior, the failure mode - before writing the prompt.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I consistently produce a structured technical context statement before AI engagement - the system state, constraints, success criteria, and what a useful output looks like. I can show this to a colleague.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have made structured pre-prompt framing a team standard. My approach to AI problem definition has been adopted by engineers on my team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O2",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you evaluate AI-generated code:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"If the code compiles, passes tests, and addresses the prompt, I generally use it. I do not systematically evaluate against the underlying engineering intent.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I sense when something is off with AI-generated code, but I cannot always articulate specifically what or why.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly evaluate AI-generated code against the original engineering intent - not just the prompt - and I can explain my acceptance or rejection reasoning to a colleague.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My AI code evaluation is systematic. I can consistently identify the class of error in AI output, articulate the specific failure in the model's reasoning, and explain why I iterated or accepted.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed code evaluation frameworks that my team now uses. My approach to AI output assessment has raised the engineering quality standard of the team's work.",s:5}]},
{id:"O3",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your AI iteration behavior when code output is insufficient:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I typically accept the first or second output. If it is not right I either accept what is there or start from scratch with a different approach.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I iterate when something is clearly wrong but I do not have a systematic approach. I stop when the output feels acceptable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I iterate with directed purpose - each iteration is guided by a specific diagnosis of what the previous output got wrong and what a better prompt should target.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My iteration is highly disciplined. I can distinguish between an iteration opportunity and a genuine architectural judgment call that requires human engineering decision rather than further AI direction.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I coach junior engineers on iteration discipline. The teams I work with produce better AI-assisted code because of how I have shaped their iteration practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"O4",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you design the role of AI in your engineering workflow:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I use AI where it clearly helps - boilerplate, documentation, test generation. I have not explicitly designed which engineering tasks AI should handle versus which require my full attention.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a general sense of where AI adds value in my engineering work, but this is intuitive rather than deliberate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before significant engineering tasks, I explicitly consider what AI will generate, what I will architect or decide, and where engineering judgment is load-bearing - and I design my workflow accordingly.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I design Human-AI engineering collaboration at the team level. I make explicit decisions about which engineering tasks AI handles across the team and where human review is mandatory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I architect Human-AI engineering division of responsibility for my organization. The frameworks I have designed for AI-assisted development governance are used beyond my immediate team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O5",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your reflective practice after AI-assisted engineering work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"After AI-assisted coding sessions I move directly to the next task. I do not typically reflect on the quality of my AI direction.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally note what worked or did not in an AI session, but this is informal and inconsistent.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly reflect on the quality of my AI direction after significant engineering engagements - what I prompted for, what I got, and what would have made my direction more precise.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My reflection practice is documented. I maintain notes on AI direction decisions that inform my engineering practice over time.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My reflection practice has produced team engineering standards for AI-assisted development that go beyond my own work.",s:5}]},
{id:"T1",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you monitor trust in your key engineering relationships:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I am generally aware when engineering relationships feel collaborative or strained, but I do not actively monitor trust state.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I notice when trust shifts significantly - a change in review tone, a withdrawal from technical discussion - but I do not track trust systematically.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively monitor whether trust in my key engineering and stakeholder relationships is growing or declining, and I can describe the trajectory of my three most important working relationships.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I track trust state systematically in my engineering relationships. I can describe the specific moments and interactions that have shaped each key relationship's trust trajectory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I design trust architecture deliberately in my engineering context. I have a clear strategy for each key relationship - with stakeholders, tech leads, and client engineers - and I adjust my behavior proactively.",s:5}]},
{id:"T2",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle AI involvement in engineering work delivered to clients or stakeholders:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I do not typically disclose which parts of my engineering output were AI-assisted unless specifically asked.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I mention AI involvement if it seems relevant to the conversation, but I do not have a consistent approach.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am intentional about communicating AI involvement in engineering deliverables - framing it in a way that maintains stakeholder confidence in the quality, security, and accountability of the work.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I proactively manage AI disclosure as a trust-building practice - designing how and when to communicate AI involvement based on what I know about each stakeholder's technical understanding and concerns.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed team-level frameworks for AI transparency in engineering delivery that others use. My approach to AI disclosure in technical work has become a standard practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"T3",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle moments when trust in an engineering relationship is at risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to avoid situations where trust might be damaged. I am more comfortable with technically smooth relationships than addressing interpersonal friction directly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I address trust ruptures when they become unavoidable, but I tend to wait until the damage is visible before responding.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I address trust risks proactively - naming tensions before they become ruptures and taking deliberate action to repair working relationships before damage compounds delivery.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at trust recovery in high-stakes engineering situations. I can repair damaged relationships with clients or engineering colleagues through deliberate, honest engagement.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My trust recovery capability in engineering contexts is recognized. I am brought into team or client relationship situations that others have been unable to repair.",s:5}]},
{id:"T4",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your behavior under engineering pressure or high-visibility production situations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Under significant engineering pressure I sometimes make decisions or communicate in ways that are not fully consistent with how I would want to be seen.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am generally consistent, but high-stakes production situations occasionally reveal gaps between how I intend to behave and how I actually behave.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am reliably consistent across high-visibility and routine engineering situations. My behavior in an incident does not differ significantly from my behavior in a regular sprint.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My consistency under engineering pressure is recognized. Colleagues trust that I will behave the same way in a production incident as in a normal engineering review.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My consistency under engineering pressure is exceptional. I have been trusted with critical production situations specifically because my decision-making and communication are predictable and principled under stress.",s:5}]},
{id:"T5",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to make AI-assisted engineering feel trustworthy to clients and non-technical stakeholders:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I have not specifically thought about how AI involvement affects the trust non-technical stakeholders have in my engineering work. I focus on technical quality.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am aware that some clients or stakeholders are uncertain about AI in engineering, but I do not have a specific approach to managing this.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively work to make AI-assisted engineering feel transparent and trustworthy - explaining AI's role, naming its limitations, and making clear where human engineering judgment provides accountability.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at introducing AI into engineering relationships where there is initial skepticism. I can shift stakeholder perception through deliberate technical communication and demonstrated quality.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed organizational approaches to AI trust-building in engineering contexts that are used beyond my own projects. My ability to make AI-assisted engineering feel safe is a recognized capability.",s:5}]},
{id:"R1",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you engage with domains outside software engineering:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I primarily work within engineering. I engage with product, design, or business domains when required but do not seek this out.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am curious about adjacent domains and occasionally explore product or business thinking, but this is informal and does not typically affect my engineering work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly and deliberately engage with domains outside engineering - product strategy, business operations, user research - because I find the distance genuinely useful for making better engineering decisions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-domain engagement is a core part of how I engineer. I have contributed meaningfully to product, design, and business decisions, using the distance between engineering and other domains as a source of architectural insight.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-domain Range is recognized and sought out. I am regularly brought into non-engineering discussions because my outside engineering perspective is valued - and the synthesis I produce across engineering and other domains is recognized as distinctive.",s:5}]},
{id:"R2",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you use knowledge from non-engineering domains in your technical work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to apply engineering thinking to engineering problems. I do not typically look for cross-domain connections.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice parallels between engineering and other domains, but I do not systematically use these observations to inform technical decisions.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly transfer mental models across domains - deliberately looking for how frameworks from business strategy, psychology, or design apply to engineering problems.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Mental model transfer is a characteristic of how I engineer. I can point to specific instances where a non-engineering insight changed how I approached a technical problem - and the system design was measurably better as a result.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to transfer mental models from other domains into engineering is recognized as a distinctive capability. I have produced architectural insights through cross-domain synthesis that purely technical engineers could not have generated.",s:5}]},
{id:"R3",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your recent contribution outside software engineering:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My recent contributions have been primarily within engineering. I have not made significant contributions to product, business, or design decisions in the last three months.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have made minor contributions outside engineering recently - attending product meetings, offering technical opinions on business decisions - but I would not describe these as substantive.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I have made at least one genuine contribution outside engineering in the last three months - one where my involvement produced a meaningfully better non-engineering outcome.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I have made multiple substantive contributions outside engineering in the last three months. The product managers, designers, or business stakeholders involved would recognize and describe the value I added.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"Contributing outside engineering is a regular, significant part of my work. The contributions I make in product, design, and business domains are recognized as among my most valuable professional contributions.",s:5}]},
{id:"R4",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to operate effectively in non-engineering contexts:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I find it difficult to contribute confidently in non-technical domains. I tend to defer to product managers or business stakeholders even when I have relevant perspective.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I can engage in non-engineering contexts, but I find it harder than technical work and I am not always sure when my engineering perspective adds genuine value versus creating friction.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I can operate in non-engineering contexts by deliberately suspending technical assumptions and approaching the domain with structured curiosity. I can usually identify where my engineering perspective adds genuine value.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at operating in non-engineering contexts. I know how to make my technical expertise relevant to business, product, or design problems without imposing engineering frameworks where they do not apply.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to operate in non-engineering contexts is exceptional. I can become a genuine contributor in product, design, or business domains rapidly - bringing structured engineering perspective that domain insiders cannot generate.",s:5}]},
{id:"R5",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how non-engineering experience affects your technical work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My non-engineering experiences are interesting but do not significantly affect how I approach engineering problems.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice that a business or product experience has changed how I think about a technical problem, but this is rare and unplanned.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Non-engineering experiences regularly change how I approach technical problems. I deliberately look for what business, design, or user contexts can teach me about engineering.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"The influence runs both ways deliberately. I use engineering thinking to illuminate business and product problems, and I use business and product thinking to challenge and improve my engineering practice.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-domain learning produces technical capabilities that pure engineering experience could not produce. This synthesis is recognized by colleagues as a genuine differentiator in how I approach complex system design.",s:5}]},
],
con:[
{id:"V1",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A client says: 'We need a market entry strategy for Southeast Asia.' The brief is high-level. Before engaging AI you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Begin researching Southeast Asian markets immediately - the request is clear enough to start.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Clarify which specific markets and entry modes the client is considering before starting.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Write a one-paragraph problem statement defining what decision this strategy will inform, what 'market entry' means specifically, and what a successful output would enable the client to do - before writing a single prompt.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the client to provide a detailed brief with constraints and priorities before you engage.",s:1}]},
{id:"V2",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"AI produces a comprehensive strategic analysis that answers the client's stated question. Reviewing it, you notice the analysis addresses the symptom - declining market share - but not the underlying structural problem causing it. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Deliver the analysis - it answers the stated question thoroughly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Add a section flagging that the root cause may be structural.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Restructure the entire analysis around the underlying structural problem - addressing the symptom will not produce durable recommendations.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present both framings to the client and let them decide which question they want answered.",s:1}]},
{id:"V3",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"You are three hours into an AI-assisted competitive analysis when you realize you have been benchmarking against the wrong competitive set - the client's real competition is from an adjacent sector, not their traditional peers. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Complete the analysis with the current competitive set - it is still useful context.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Complete the analysis but add a section on the adjacent sector competition.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Start over with the correct competitive frame - a well-executed analysis of the wrong competitive set will lead the client to the wrong strategic conclusions.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present both competitive frames to the client and let them decide which is more relevant.",s:1}]},
{id:"V4",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A junior analyst asks you to review their AI-assisted client deliverable before submission. The analysis is technically correct but addresses a strategic question that the client has moved past - their priorities shifted in the last two weeks. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Approve it - the analysis is correct and the brief was set before the priorities shifted.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Flag the disconnect as a comment and let them decide whether to update it.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Sit with them, reframe around what the client actually needs now, and restructure the analysis together - treating this as a client-first thinking development moment.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reach out to the client directly to confirm current priorities before any work is submitted.",s:1}]},
{id:"V5",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your consulting firm is about to invest significant resources in a client engagement based on a problem definition set in the proposal three months ago. You believe the problem definition is now materially wrong - the client's situation has changed - and that excellent execution of the wrong problem will damage the relationship. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Execute the engagement as scoped - the problem was agreed and reopening it creates fee and timeline risk.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise your concern informally with the engagement partner.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Formally recommend reframing the engagement scope with a documented rationale - accepting the commercial friction this creates and taking accountability for the disruption.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Execute the current scope but design an interim check-in where the problem definition can be reviewed.",s:1}]},
{id:"E1",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted strategic recommendations to a senior client in 45 minutes. The analysis is sound but one recommendation feels directionally wrong given what you know about this client's culture - you cannot immediately articulate why. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the full recommendations - the analysis is sound and the instinct may be wrong.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Remove the recommendation that feels wrong and present the remaining ones without flagging the gap.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Spend the 45 minutes specifically testing what feels wrong against everything you know about the client - then either revise it or present it with an explicit caveat naming the cultural tension.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Postpone the presentation until you can validate the recommendation with someone who knows the client better.",s:1}]},
{id:"E2",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"Your team has been pursuing an AI-recommended strategic hypothesis for three weeks. Client data is not confirming the hypothesis, but the model continues to support it with high confidence. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue pursuing the hypothesis - three weeks of client data is not enough to override a high-confidence model.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise the tension with the engagement partner and defer to their call.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call to abandon the hypothesis based on your reading of the client context - document your reasoning and take accountability for the pivot.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Commission additional client interviews to build a stronger evidence base before deciding.",s:1}]},
{id:"E3",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are leading a strategy engagement and AI analysis recommends a course of action that contradicts the direction your most senior client stakeholder expects. The AI model's confidence level is high. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the AI recommendation as-is - high confidence is high confidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Soften the presentation of the recommendation to reduce stakeholder friction.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the recommendation clearly, explain your own assessment of why it contradicts expectations, and invite the stakeholder to engage with the tension directly.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reframe the analysis with different assumptions until the output aligns with stakeholder expectations.",s:1}]},
{id:"E4",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are the most senior person available to make a consequential recommendation to a client. You have 60% of the data you would want. Waiting three days for the remaining data will miss the client's decision window. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Wait the three days - strategic recommendations require adequate evidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Escalate to the engagement partner so a more senior person takes accountability for the recommendation.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the recommendation with 60% data, document your reasoning and assumptions explicitly, name the conditions that would cause you to revise it, and take full accountability.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Make a provisional recommendation and communicate it explicitly as subject to revision when the remaining data arrives.",s:1}]},
{id:"E5",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"An AI-assisted market sizing model your team has used for two months has a systematic error that means some client-facing outputs were materially wrong. You do not yet know which clients were affected. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Quietly correct the model and audit all outputs internally before informing anyone.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Inform the engagement partner and let leadership decide how to handle client disclosure.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Immediately disclose the error to all potentially affected clients, even before the full audit is complete - accepting the reputational cost in exchange for avoiding the trust cost of delayed disclosure.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Complete the full audit first, then disclose only to clients whose outputs were actually affected.",s:1}]},
{id:"C1",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are facilitating a client strategy workshop. The CEO agrees with every strategic proposal quickly and enthusiastically. The junior client team members stay largely silent. As the workshop ends you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Feel satisfied - CEO endorsement is the most important signal in a strategy workshop.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Note the dynamic and plan to follow up with the junior team members separately.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize that rapid CEO agreement combined with junior silence often signals compliance - and before the workshop closes, create a specific moment for junior team members to surface concerns or alternative views.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the CEO directly whether the team shares their enthusiasm.",s:1}]},
{id:"C2",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"In a client team meeting, two senior client executives disagree sharply about strategic direction. One concedes within minutes and the meeting moves on. Afterward you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the agreed direction - the disagreement was resolved.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in with the executive who conceded to make sure they are comfortable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Note that the speed of concession is unlikely to reflect genuine strategic alignment - and find a private moment to surface whether the unresolved tension will affect implementation before the strategy is finalized.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the dynamic with the engagement partner so they are aware.",s:1}]},
{id:"C3",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"A key client contact - previously highly engaged and forthcoming - has become noticeably cooler over the past two weeks. They still attend meetings and answer questions but volunteer no information and have stopped pushing back on recommendations. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Note no concern - they are still participating in the engagement.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in briefly to ask if everything is going well.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize the behavioral shift as a significant engagement signal - find a private moment to genuinely understand what has changed, without presupposing what you will hear.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the change with the engagement partner as a potential relationship risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"C4",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting strategic options to the client's executive team. The analysis clearly supports one option. The CFO is signaling through tone and body language - not words - that they have a strong preference for a different option. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the analytically supported recommendation regardless of the signal - the analysis should drive the decision.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust your recommendation to leave room for the CFO's implicit preference.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the analytically supported recommendation clearly, then address the tension directly: 'I notice there may be a different perspective on this - I'd like to understand it before we make a final recommendation.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Speak with the CFO privately before the presentation to understand their preference and adjust the recommendation accordingly.",s:1}]},
{id:"C5",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You have been engaged to assess a client organization's readiness for a major strategic transformation. Senior leadership presents a confident, unified narrative. After two days of interviews with mid-management, you sense significant uncertainty, skepticism, and passive resistance beneath the official narrative. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Report on the official narrative - your brief was to assess readiness and leadership has confirmed commitment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Include a brief note that 'some concerns were raised at operational levels.'",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present a candid assessment that distinguishes the official narrative from the organizational reality - naming the gaps with specific evidence from your interviews.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Share your findings with senior leadership verbally before writing the report to test how much candor they want in the document.",s:1}]},
{id:"O1",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you approach AI assistance on significant client analysis or strategy work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I enter the client problem or research question into AI and work with what comes back. I refine if the first output is not useful.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a rough sense of what I need before prompting, but I rarely write this down or structure it explicitly.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before engaging AI on a significant client problem, I write a clear problem statement - the strategic question, the client context, the decision it will inform - before writing the prompt.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I consistently produce a structured client context brief before AI engagement - the question, the client situation, the constraints, and what a useful output would enable. I can show this to a colleague.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have made structured pre-prompt framing a team standard. My approach to AI problem definition for client work has been adopted by consultants on my engagement team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O2",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you evaluate AI-generated strategic analysis or recommendations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"If the analysis looks comprehensive and addresses the prompt, I generally use it. I do not systematically evaluate against the underlying strategic question.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I sense when something is off with AI-generated analysis, but I cannot always articulate specifically what or why.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly evaluate AI-generated analysis against the original strategic question - not just the prompt - and I can explain my acceptance or rejection reasoning to a colleague.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My AI output evaluation for client work is systematic. I can consistently identify the type of reasoning error in AI output and articulate specifically why I iterated or accepted.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed analysis evaluation frameworks for AI-assisted client work that my team now uses. My approach to AI output quality has raised the standard of our consulting deliverables.",s:5}]},
{id:"O3",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your AI iteration behavior when strategic output is insufficient:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I typically accept the first or second output for client work. If it is not right I either use what is there or approach the problem differently.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I iterate when something is clearly wrong but I do not have a systematic approach. I stop when the output feels adequate for client purposes.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I iterate with directed purpose on client work - each iteration is guided by a specific diagnosis of what the previous output got wrong strategically.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My iteration discipline on client work is high. I can distinguish between an iteration opportunity and a genuine strategic judgment call that requires consulting expertise rather than further AI direction.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I coach junior consultants on AI iteration discipline for client work. My engagements produce better AI-assisted analysis because of how I have shaped the team's iteration practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"O4",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you design the role of AI in your consulting workflow:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I use AI where it clearly helps in consulting - research, synthesis, slide drafts. I have not explicitly designed which consulting tasks AI should handle versus which require my full strategic attention.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a general sense of where AI adds value in consulting work, but this is intuitive rather than deliberate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before significant client work, I explicitly consider what AI will research or generate, what strategic thinking I will provide, and where consulting judgment is load-bearing - and I design my workflow accordingly.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I design Human-AI consulting collaboration at the engagement level. I make explicit decisions about which consulting tasks AI handles across the team and where senior consultant review is mandatory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I architect Human-AI division of consulting work for my practice. The frameworks I have designed for AI-assisted consulting governance are used beyond my immediate engagement team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O5",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your reflective practice after AI-assisted client work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"After AI-assisted consulting work I move directly to the next task. I do not typically reflect on the quality of my AI direction.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally note what worked or did not in an AI session for client work, but this is informal and inconsistent.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly reflect on the quality of my AI direction after significant client engagements - what I prompted for, what I got, and what would have made my direction more strategic.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My reflection practice on AI-assisted client work is documented. I maintain notes on AI direction decisions that inform how I approach similar problems for future clients.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My reflection practice has produced consulting team standards for AI-assisted client work that go beyond my own engagements.",s:5}]},
{id:"T1",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you monitor trust in your key client relationships:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I am generally aware when client relationships feel engaged or strained, but I do not actively monitor trust state.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I notice when client trust shifts significantly - a change in communication tone, a withdrawal from meetings - but I do not track trust systematically.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively monitor whether trust in my key client relationships is growing or declining, and I can describe the current trajectory of my three most important client relationships.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I track client trust state systematically. I can describe the specific moments and interactions that have shaped each key client relationship's trust trajectory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I design client trust architecture deliberately. I have a clear strategy for each key client relationship and I adjust my engagement behavior proactively - not just in response to shifts I observe.",s:5}]},
{id:"T2",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle AI involvement in client deliverables:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I do not typically disclose which parts of my client deliverables were AI-assisted unless specifically asked.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I mention AI involvement if it seems relevant to the client conversation, but I do not have a consistent approach.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am intentional about communicating AI involvement in client deliverables - framing it in a way that maintains client confidence in the quality, integrity, and accountability of the consulting work.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I proactively manage AI disclosure as a trust-building practice with clients - designing how and when to communicate AI involvement based on what I know about each client's expectations and concerns.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed practice-level frameworks for AI transparency in client delivery that others use. My approach to AI disclosure has become a standard in my consulting team.",s:5}]},
{id:"T3",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle moments when trust in a client relationship is at risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to avoid situations where client trust might be damaged. I am more comfortable with smooth client relationships than addressing friction directly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I address client trust ruptures when they become unavoidable, but I tend to wait until the damage is visible before responding.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I address client trust risks proactively - naming tensions before they become ruptures and taking deliberate action to repair relationships before damage compounds.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at trust recovery in high-stakes client situations. I can repair damaged client relationships through deliberate, honest engagement even when the initial cause of damage was significant.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My client trust recovery capability is recognized. I am brought into relationship situations that other consultants have been unable to repair.",s:5}]},
{id:"T4",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your behavior under client pressure or high-visibility situations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Under significant client pressure I sometimes behave in ways that are not fully consistent with how I would want to be seen.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am generally consistent, but high-stakes client situations occasionally reveal gaps between how I intend to behave and how I actually behave.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am reliably consistent across high-visibility and routine client interactions. My behavior with a C-suite client does not differ significantly from my behavior in a working team session.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My consistency under client pressure is recognized. Clients and colleagues trust that I will behave the same way in a difficult client meeting as in a routine update.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My consistency under client pressure is exceptional. I have been trusted with the most difficult client situations specifically because my behavior is predictable and principled under stress.",s:5}]},
{id:"T5",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to make AI-assisted consulting feel trustworthy to clients:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I have not specifically thought about how AI involvement affects the trust clients have in my consulting work. I focus on the quality of the strategic output.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am aware that some clients are uncertain about AI in consulting, but I do not have a specific approach to managing this.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively work to make AI-assisted consulting feel transparent and trustworthy - explaining AI's role, naming its limitations, and making clear where consulting judgment provides the accountability.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at introducing AI into client relationships where there is initial skepticism. I can shift client perception through deliberate communication and demonstrated quality.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed practice-level approaches to AI trust-building with clients that are used beyond my own engagements. My ability to make AI-assisted consulting feel credible is a recognized capability.",s:5}]},
{id:"R1",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you engage with domains outside your primary consulting practice area:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I primarily work within my practice area. I engage with other sectors or functions when required but do not seek this out.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am curious about adjacent practice areas and occasionally explore them, but this is informal and does not typically affect my consulting work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly and deliberately engage with domains outside my primary practice area because I find the distance genuinely useful for generating more creative strategic insight.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-domain engagement is a core part of how I consult. I have contributed meaningfully to engagements outside my primary practice and actively use the distance between sectors as a source of strategic insight.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-domain Range is recognized and sought out. I am regularly brought into engagements outside my primary practice because my outside perspective generates strategic options that sector specialists cannot see.",s:5}]},
{id:"R2",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you use knowledge from outside your primary practice area in client work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to apply practice area thinking to practice area problems. I do not typically look for cross-sector connections.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice parallels between my practice area and other sectors, but I do not systematically use these observations in client work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly transfer frameworks and patterns across sectors - deliberately looking for how strategic approaches from one industry apply to a client's problem in another.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-sector mental model transfer is a characteristic of how I consult. I can point to specific instances where an insight from outside the client's sector changed the strategic direction of an engagement.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to transfer strategic frameworks across sectors is recognized as a distinctive consulting capability. I have produced strategic insights through cross-sector synthesis that sector specialists could not have generated.",s:5}]},
{id:"R3",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your recent contribution outside your primary consulting practice area:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My recent contributions have been primarily within my practice area. I have not made significant contributions to engagements outside my primary sector in the last three months.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have made minor contributions outside my practice area recently - attending cross-practice meetings, offering sector opinions - but I would not describe these as substantive.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I have made at least one genuine contribution outside my primary practice area in the last three months - one where my involvement produced a meaningfully better strategic outcome.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I have made multiple substantive contributions outside my primary practice area in the last three months. The clients or colleagues involved would recognize and describe the value I added.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"Contributing outside my primary practice area is a regular, significant part of my consulting work. The contributions I make in other sectors or functions are recognized as among my most valuable professional contributions.",s:5}]},
{id:"R4",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to operate effectively in sectors or functions outside your primary consulting expertise:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I find it difficult to contribute confidently in sectors outside my primary practice area. I tend to defer to sector specialists even when I have relevant strategic perspective.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I can engage in engagements outside my primary practice area, but I find it harder than primary area work and I am not always sure when my perspective adds genuine value.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I can operate in engagements outside my primary practice area by deliberately suspending sector-specific assumptions and approaching the new context with structured strategic curiosity.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at operating in engagements outside my primary practice area. I know how to make my strategic expertise relevant in new sectors without imposing frameworks where they do not apply.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to operate in sectors outside my primary practice area is exceptional. I can become a genuine strategic contributor in a new sector rapidly - bringing structured outside perspective that sector specialists cannot generate.",s:5}]},
{id:"R5",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how cross-sector experience affects your primary consulting practice:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My cross-sector experiences are interesting but do not significantly affect how I approach problems in my primary practice area.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice that a cross-sector engagement has changed how I think about a problem in my primary practice, but this is rare and unplanned.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Cross-sector experiences regularly change how I consult in my primary practice area. I deliberately look for what other sectors can teach me and apply these transfers consciously.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"The influence runs both ways deliberately. I use my primary practice to illuminate problems in other sectors, and I use other sectors to challenge and improve my primary practice consulting.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-sector learning produces strategic capabilities that single-sector experience could not produce. This synthesis is recognized by clients and colleagues as a genuine consulting differentiator.",s:5}]},
],
fin:[
{id:"V1",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A senior leader asks for 'an analysis of our cost base.' The brief is open-ended. Before engaging AI you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Begin the cost analysis immediately - the request is clear enough to start.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Clarify whether they want actuals, forecasts, benchmarks, or variance analysis before starting.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Write a one-paragraph problem statement defining what decision this analysis will inform, what 'cost base' means in this context, and what a useful output would enable the leader to do - before writing a single prompt.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the leader to provide a detailed brief with the specific metrics and time periods they need before you engage.",s:1}]},
{id:"V2",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"AI produces a thorough financial model that answers the stated question. Reviewing it, you notice the model is technically correct but optimizes for cost minimization when the real goal should be optimizing risk-adjusted return. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Deliver the model - it answers the stated question correctly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Add a note flagging that a risk-adjusted return perspective might be more appropriate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Rebuild the model around the correct objective - an optimized solution to the wrong objective will drive the wrong decisions.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present both versions - cost-minimized and risk-adjusted - and let the senior leader decide which objective to optimize for.",s:1}]},
{id:"V3",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"You are three hours into an AI-assisted financial analysis when you realize you have been analyzing the wrong business unit - the data pull was from a subsidiary rather than the parent entity. Starting over means losing the three hours. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Complete the analysis with the current data - the subsidiary data still provides useful context.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Complete the subsidiary analysis and add a note clarifying the data scope.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Start over with the correct entity - financial analysis with the wrong scope will produce materially misleading conclusions for any decision based on it.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present the subsidiary analysis with a clear label and let the requestor decide whether it is adequate.",s:1}]},
{id:"V4",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A junior finance analyst asks you to review their AI-assisted financial model before it is presented to the CFO. The model is technically correct but answers a question the CFO asked six weeks ago - the business priorities have shifted materially since then. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Approve it - the model is correct and the brief was set when the priorities were as stated.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Flag the disconnect as a review comment and let the analyst decide whether to update it.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Sit with them, identify what the CFO actually needs to make decisions today, and restructure the model together - treating this as a financial judgment development moment.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reach out to the CFO's office to confirm current priorities before any model is presented.",s:1}]},
{id:"V5",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your finance function is about to invest significant effort in building an AI-assisted forecasting system based on assumptions set at the start of the year. You believe the macroeconomic assumptions underpinning the system are now materially wrong - deploying a sophisticated system built on wrong assumptions will produce confident but misleading forecasts. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Build the system as scoped - the assumptions were agreed and reopening them creates delay.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise your concern informally with the finance director.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Formally challenge the assumptions with documented evidence and a recommended update - accepting the delay this creates and taking accountability for the disruption.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Build the system as scoped but design it with a clearly visible assumption log that makes the sensitivity to macro conditions transparent.",s:1}]},
{id:"E1",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted financial projections to the CFO in 45 minutes. The model is internally consistent but one revenue assumption feels directionally wrong based on what you know about the market - you cannot immediately quantify why. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the projections as modelled - they are internally consistent and the instinct may be wrong.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Remove the revenue line that feels wrong and present the remaining projections without flagging the gap.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Spend the 45 minutes specifically stress-testing the assumption that feels wrong - then either correct it or present the projections with a named sensitivity analysis on that assumption.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Postpone the presentation until you can validate the revenue assumption with market data.",s:1}]},
{id:"E2",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"Your team has been using an AI-driven risk model for three weeks. It is producing risk scores that feel systematically underweighted for a particular exposure class. The model continues to assign low risk scores with high confidence. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue using the model - three weeks is not enough to override a high-confidence risk assessment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise the concern with the head of risk and defer to their judgment.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call to flag the exposure class for manual review and document your reasoning - taking accountability for the additional scrutiny this creates.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Commission a model validation review before deciding whether to override the risk scores.",s:1}]},
{id:"E3",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted financial analysis that contradicts the direction your most senior finance stakeholder expects based on prior year performance. The model's confidence is high. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the analysis as-is - high confidence is high confidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust the presentation to reduce the contrast with prior year expectations.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the analysis clearly, explain your assessment of why it diverges from prior year, and invite the stakeholder to engage with the tension directly.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Rerun the model with different parameter assumptions until the output aligns with prior year expectations.",s:1}]},
{id:"E4",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are the most senior finance professional available to make a consequential investment or budget decision. You have 60% of the financial data you would want. The decision window closes in three days. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Wait for the remaining data - consequential financial decisions require adequate information.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Escalate to the CFO so a more senior person takes accountability for the decision.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the recommendation with 60% data, document your assumptions and the conditions that would cause you to revise it, and take full accountability.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Make a provisional recommendation and communicate it explicitly as subject to revision when the remaining data is available.",s:1}]},
{id:"E5",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"An AI-assisted financial reporting tool your team has used for two months has a systematic error that means some management reports contain materially wrong figures. You do not yet know which reports are affected. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Quietly correct the tool and audit all reports internally before informing anyone.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Inform the finance director and let leadership decide how to handle disclosure.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Immediately disclose the error to all potentially affected stakeholders - including senior leadership and any external parties who received the reports - even before the full audit is complete.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Complete the full audit first, then disclose only to stakeholders whose specific reports were materially affected.",s:1}]},
{id:"C1",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting a financial plan to the executive team. The CFO endorses every assumption quickly and enthusiastically. The business unit finance leads stay largely silent. As the meeting ends you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Feel satisfied - CFO endorsement is the most important signal for a financial plan.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Note the dynamic and plan to follow up with the business unit finance leads separately.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize that rapid CFO endorsement combined with business unit silence often signals compliance or unspoken concern about the assumptions - and before the meeting closes, create a specific moment for business unit leads to raise challenges.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the CFO directly whether the business unit teams share their view.",s:1}]},
{id:"C2",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"In a budget review meeting, two senior leaders disagree sharply about a capital allocation decision. One concedes within minutes and the review moves on. Afterward you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the agreed allocation - the disagreement was resolved.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in with the leader who conceded to make sure they are comfortable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Note that the speed of concession is unlikely to reflect genuine alignment on the capital allocation - and find a private moment to surface whether the unresolved tension will affect the budget's implementation.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the dynamic with the CFO so they are aware.",s:1}]},
{id:"C3",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"A business partner who has always been highly engaged with finance - sharing context, pushing back on numbers - has become noticeably less forthcoming over the past three weeks. They still attend financial review meetings but contribute minimally. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Note no concern - they are still attending meetings.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in briefly to ask if everything is going well.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize the behavioral shift as a meaningful signal - find a private moment to genuinely understand what has changed, without presupposing what you will hear.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the change with the finance director as a potential business relationship risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"C4",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting investment options to the board. The financial data clearly supports one option. The board chair is signaling through tone and body language - not words - that they have a strong preference for a different option. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the financially supported recommendation regardless of the signal - the data should drive the decision.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust your recommendation to leave room for the board chair's implicit preference.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the financially supported recommendation clearly, then address the tension directly: 'I notice there may be a different perspective on this - I'd like to understand it before we make a recommendation.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Speak with the board chair privately before the presentation to understand their preference and adjust the analysis accordingly.",s:1}]},
{id:"C5",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You have been engaged to assess a portfolio company's financial health. Senior management presents a confident narrative of strong performance. After two days of reviewing data and speaking with finance team members, you sense significant tension around cash flow management and aggressive accounting judgments beneath the official narrative. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Report on the official management narrative - your brief was to assess financial health and management has confirmed strong performance.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Include a brief note that 'some areas warrant further scrutiny.'",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present a candid financial assessment that distinguishes the official narrative from the financial reality - naming the cash flow tensions and accounting judgments with evidence.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Share your observations with the portfolio company's management verbally before writing the report to understand how much candor they want in the document.",s:1}]},
{id:"O1",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you approach AI assistance on significant financial analysis or modelling work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I enter the financial question or data request into AI and work with what comes back. I refine if the first output is not useful.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a rough sense of what I need before prompting, but I rarely write this down or structure it explicitly.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before engaging AI on a significant financial analysis, I write a clear problem definition - the financial question, the decision it will inform, the data constraints - before writing the prompt.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I consistently produce a structured financial context brief before AI engagement - the question, the entity scope, the time period, the assumptions, and what a useful output would look like. I can show this to a colleague.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have made structured pre-prompt framing a team standard for financial work. My approach to AI problem definition has been adopted by analysts on my finance team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O2",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you evaluate AI-generated financial analysis or models:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"If the financial output looks internally consistent and addresses the prompt, I generally use it. I do not systematically evaluate against the underlying financial question.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I sense when something is off with AI-generated financial analysis, but I cannot always articulate specifically what or why.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly evaluate AI-generated financial output against the original question - not just the prompt - and I can explain my acceptance or rejection reasoning to a colleague.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My AI financial output evaluation is systematic. I can consistently identify the type of modelling or analytical error in AI output and articulate specifically why I iterated or accepted.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed financial analysis evaluation frameworks for AI-assisted work that my team now uses. My approach to AI output quality has raised the standard of our financial analysis.",s:5}]},
{id:"O3",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your AI iteration behavior when financial analysis output is insufficient:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I typically accept the first or second output for financial work. If it is not right I either use what is there or rebuild the model manually.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I iterate when something is clearly wrong in the financial output but I do not have a systematic approach. I stop when the output feels adequate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I iterate with directed purpose on financial work - each iteration is guided by a specific diagnosis of what the previous output got wrong analytically.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My iteration discipline on financial analysis is high. I can distinguish between an iteration opportunity and a genuine financial judgment call that requires professional expertise rather than further AI direction.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I coach junior finance analysts on AI iteration discipline. My team produces better AI-assisted financial analysis because of how I have shaped the iteration practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"O4",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you design the role of AI in your financial workflow:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I use AI where it clearly helps in finance - data extraction, variance analysis, report drafts. I have not explicitly designed which finance tasks AI should handle versus which require my professional judgment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a general sense of where AI adds value in finance work, but this is intuitive rather than deliberate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before significant financial work, I explicitly consider what AI will compute or generate, what financial judgment I will provide, and where professional finance expertise is load-bearing - and I design accordingly.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I design Human-AI finance collaboration at the team level. I make explicit decisions about which financial tasks AI handles and where qualified professional review is mandatory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I architect Human-AI division of finance work for my function. The frameworks I have designed for AI-assisted financial work governance are used beyond my immediate team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O5",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your reflective practice after AI-assisted financial work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"After AI-assisted financial analysis I move directly to the next task. I do not typically reflect on the quality of my AI direction.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally note what worked or did not in an AI session for financial work, but this is informal and inconsistent.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly reflect on the quality of my AI direction after significant financial work - what I prompted for, what I got, and what would have made my direction more precise.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My reflection practice on AI-assisted financial work is documented. I maintain notes on AI direction decisions that inform how I approach similar analyses in the future.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My reflection practice has produced finance team standards for AI-assisted financial analysis that go beyond my own work.",s:5}]},
{id:"T1",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you monitor trust in your key finance business partnerships:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I am generally aware when finance business partnerships feel collaborative or strained, but I do not actively monitor trust state.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I notice when trust shifts significantly in a finance relationship - a change in information sharing, a withdrawal from collaborative planning - but I do not track trust systematically.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively monitor whether trust in my key finance business partnerships is growing or declining, and I can describe the trajectory of my three most important relationships.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I track trust state systematically in my finance relationships. I can describe the specific moments and interactions that have shaped each key relationship's trust trajectory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I design trust architecture deliberately in my finance context. I have a clear strategy for each key business partnership and I adjust my behavior proactively.",s:5}]},
{id:"T2",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle AI involvement in financial reports or analysis delivered to stakeholders:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I do not typically disclose which parts of my financial outputs were AI-assisted unless specifically asked.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I mention AI involvement if it seems relevant to the conversation, but I do not have a consistent approach.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am intentional about communicating AI involvement in financial deliverables - framing it in a way that maintains stakeholder confidence in the quality, accuracy, and professional accountability of the work.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I proactively manage AI disclosure as a trust-building practice - designing how and when to communicate AI involvement based on what I know about each stakeholder's expectations around financial rigour.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed team-level frameworks for AI transparency in financial reporting that others use. My approach to AI disclosure in finance has become a standard practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"T3",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle moments when trust in a finance business partnership is at risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to avoid situations where trust in finance relationships might be damaged. I am more comfortable with smooth relationships than addressing financial or interpersonal friction directly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I address trust ruptures in finance relationships when they become unavoidable, but I tend to wait until the damage is visible before responding.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I address finance relationship trust risks proactively - naming tensions before they become ruptures and taking deliberate action to repair relationships before damage compounds.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at trust recovery in high-stakes finance relationship situations. I can repair damaged relationships with business leaders or stakeholders through deliberate, honest engagement.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My trust recovery capability in finance relationships is recognized. I am brought into relationship situations that other finance professionals have been unable to repair.",s:5}]},
{id:"T4",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your behavior under financial pressure or high-visibility reporting situations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Under significant financial pressure or board-level scrutiny I sometimes behave in ways not fully consistent with how I would want to be seen.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am generally consistent, but high-stakes financial situations occasionally reveal gaps between how I intend to behave and how I actually behave.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am reliably consistent across high-visibility financial reporting and routine finance work. My behavior in a board presentation does not differ significantly from my behavior in a working team session.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My consistency under financial pressure is recognized. Stakeholders trust that I will present financial reality the same way in a difficult board conversation as in a routine management update.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My consistency under financial pressure is exceptional. I have been trusted with the most difficult financial disclosures specifically because my behavior is predictable and principled under scrutiny.",s:5}]},
{id:"T5",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to make AI-assisted financial work feel trustworthy to non-finance stakeholders:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I have not specifically thought about how AI involvement affects the trust non-finance stakeholders have in my financial work. I focus on accuracy.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am aware that some stakeholders are uncertain about AI in financial analysis, but I do not have a specific approach to managing this.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively work to make AI-assisted financial work feel transparent and trustworthy - explaining AI's role, naming its limitations, and making clear where professional financial judgment provides accountability.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at introducing AI into finance relationships where there is initial skepticism. I can shift stakeholder perception through deliberate communication and demonstrated analytical quality.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed approaches to AI trust-building in finance contexts that are used beyond my own work. My ability to make AI-assisted financial analysis feel credible is a recognized organizational capability.",s:5}]},
{id:"R1",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you engage with domains outside finance and risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I primarily work within finance and risk. I engage with other business functions when required but do not seek this out.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am curious about adjacent business functions and occasionally engage with operations or strategy, but this is informal and does not typically affect my finance work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly and deliberately engage with domains outside finance - commercial strategy, operations, technology - because I find the distance genuinely useful for making better financial decisions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-functional engagement is a core part of how I practice finance. I have contributed meaningfully to commercial, operational, and strategic decisions, using the distance between finance and other functions as a source of analytical insight.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-functional Range is recognized. I am regularly brought into non-finance decisions because my outside finance perspective generates insights that functional specialists cannot see.",s:5}]},
{id:"R2",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you use knowledge from outside finance in your financial work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to apply finance thinking to finance problems. I do not typically look for cross-functional connections.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice parallels between finance and other functions, but I do not systematically use these observations to inform my financial analysis.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly transfer frameworks across functions - deliberately looking for how commercial, operational, or strategic thinking applies to financial questions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-functional mental model transfer is a characteristic of how I practice finance. I can point to specific instances where an insight from outside finance changed the quality of a financial recommendation.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to transfer frameworks from other functions into financial analysis is recognized as a distinctive capability. I have produced financial insights through cross-functional synthesis that purely finance-trained analysts could not have generated.",s:5}]},
{id:"R3",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your recent contribution outside finance and risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My recent contributions have been primarily within finance and risk. I have not made significant contributions to commercial, operational, or strategic decisions in the last three months.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have made minor contributions outside finance recently - attending commercial meetings, offering financial opinions on operational decisions - but I would not describe these as substantive.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I have made at least one genuine contribution outside finance in the last three months - one where my involvement produced a meaningfully better business outcome.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I have made multiple substantive contributions outside finance in the last three months. The commercial, operational, or strategic stakeholders involved would recognize and describe the value I added.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"Contributing outside finance is a regular, significant part of my work. The contributions I make in commercial, operational, and strategic domains are recognized as among my most valuable professional contributions.",s:5}]},
{id:"R4",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to operate effectively in non-finance business contexts:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I find it difficult to contribute confidently in non-finance business contexts. I tend to defer to commercial or operational stakeholders even when I have relevant financial perspective.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I can engage in non-finance business contexts, but I find it harder than finance work and I am not always sure when my financial perspective adds genuine value versus creating complexity.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I can operate in non-finance business contexts by deliberately suspending finance-first assumptions and approaching the domain with structured business curiosity.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at operating in non-finance business contexts. I know how to make my financial expertise relevant to commercial, operational, or strategic problems without imposing finance frameworks where they do not apply.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to operate in non-finance business contexts is exceptional. I can become a genuine contributor to commercial, operational, or strategic decisions rapidly - bringing financial perspective that business specialists cannot generate themselves.",s:5}]},
{id:"R5",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how non-finance experience affects your financial work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My non-finance experiences are interesting but do not significantly affect how I approach financial analysis.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice that a commercial or operational experience has changed how I think about a financial problem, but this is rare and unplanned.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Non-finance experiences regularly change how I approach financial analysis. I deliberately look for what commercial and operational contexts can teach me about financial decision-making.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"The influence runs both ways deliberately. I use financial thinking to illuminate commercial and operational problems, and I use commercial and operational experience to improve my financial analysis.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-functional learning produces financial capabilities that purely finance-trained experience could not produce. This synthesis is recognized by colleagues as a genuine differentiator in how I approach complex financial decisions.",s:5}]},
],
hr:[
{id:"V1",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A business leader says: 'We need a learning programme to build AI capability.' Before engaging AI to design it you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Begin designing the learning programme immediately - the request is clear enough to start.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Clarify which roles and skill gaps they are most concerned about before starting.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Write a one-paragraph problem statement defining what capability gap this programme will close, what success looks like for the business, and what a useful output would enable the organization to do - before designing a single module.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the business leader to provide a detailed learning needs analysis before you engage.",s:1}]},
{id:"V2",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"AI produces a comprehensive competency framework that answers the stated talent brief. Reviewing it, you notice the framework maps skills correctly but does not address the underlying cultural and behavioral shift the organization actually needs. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Deliver the competency framework - it answers the stated brief thoroughly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Add a section noting that cultural and behavioral factors should also be considered.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Redesign the framework around the behavioral shift the organization actually needs - a technically correct framework that misses the cultural dimension will not drive real change.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present both versions - the skills-based framework and a broader behavioral framework - and let the leader decide which to use.",s:1}]},
{id:"V3",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"You are three hours into AI-assisted talent data analysis when you realize you have been analysing the wrong cohort - the data includes leavers from the previous year rather than current employees. Starting over means losing the three hours. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Complete the analysis with the current data - historical leaver patterns still provide useful context.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Complete the analysis but clearly label it as including historical leaver data.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Start over with the correct cohort - talent analysis with the wrong population will produce misleading conclusions for any HR decision based on it.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present the analysis with the current data and a note about the cohort composition, and let the business decide whether it is adequate.",s:1}]},
{id:"V4",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A junior HR analyst asks you to review their AI-assisted engagement survey analysis before it is presented to senior leadership. The analysis is technically correct but draws conclusions about engagement drivers that are no longer relevant - the organizational context shifted significantly after the survey was fielded. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Approve it - the analysis is correct given the data and the brief as set.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Flag the context change as a comment and let the analyst decide whether to update the conclusions.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Sit with them, reframe the analysis around what the data means given the current organizational context, and rebuild the narrative together.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reach out to senior leadership to confirm they are aware of the context change before the analysis is presented.",s:1}]},
{id:"V5",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your HR function is about to invest significant effort in an AI-assisted succession planning system based on a leadership model developed three years ago. You believe the leadership model is now misaligned with the capabilities the organization will actually need - identifying successors against the wrong model will produce a leadership pipeline perfectly designed for the past. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Build the system as scoped - the leadership model was agreed and reopening it creates delay.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise your concern informally with the CHRO.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Formally recommend revisiting the leadership model before building the succession system - accepting the delay this creates and taking accountability for the disruption.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Build the system as scoped but design it with an explicit model review built into the annual succession cycle.",s:1}]},
{id:"E1",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted talent analytics to the CHRO in 45 minutes. The analysis is robust but one workforce trend feels directionally wrong based on what you know from your conversations with the business - you cannot immediately identify why the data shows something different. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the analysis as modelled - it is data-driven and the qualitative instinct may be wrong.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Remove the trend that feels wrong and present the remaining findings without flagging the gap.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Spend the 45 minutes specifically testing the tension between the data and your qualitative read - then either resolve it or present the finding with a named caveat explaining the discrepancy.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Postpone the presentation until you can do additional qualitative research to validate the data trend.",s:1}]},
{id:"E2",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"Your team has been using an AI-driven talent assessment model for three weeks. It is consistently scoring a high-performing population as lower potential than your qualitative observations suggest. The model continues to assign low potential scores with high confidence. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue using the model - three weeks of qualitative observation is not enough to override a high-confidence assessment model.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise the concern with the talent director and defer to their judgment.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call to flag the population for calibration review and document your reasoning - taking accountability for questioning the model.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Commission a model validation review before deciding whether to override the scores.",s:1}]},
{id:"E3",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted workforce planning analysis that contradicts what your most senior business stakeholder expects based on their operational experience. The model confidence is high. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the analysis as-is - high confidence is high confidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Soften the presentation to reduce the contrast with the stakeholder's operational experience.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the analysis clearly, explain your HR read of why it diverges from operational experience, and invite the stakeholder to engage with the tension.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reframe the analysis with different assumptions until the output aligns with the stakeholder's operational experience.",s:1}]},
{id:"E4",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are the most senior HR professional available to make a consequential talent decision - a promotion, a performance action, or a key hire. You have 60% of the people data you would want. The decision window closes in three days. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Wait for the remaining data - consequential people decisions require adequate information.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Escalate to the CHRO so a more senior person takes accountability for the decision.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call with 60% data, document your reasoning and the conditions that would cause you to revise it, and take full accountability.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Make a provisional decision and communicate it explicitly as subject to review when the remaining information is available.",s:1}]},
{id:"E5",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"An AI-assisted HR analytics tool your team has used for two months has been systematically misclassifying a group of employees in ways that have affected their development recommendations. You do not yet know how many employees are affected. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Quietly correct the classification and audit all records internally before informing anyone.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Inform the CHRO and let HR leadership decide how to handle disclosure.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Immediately disclose the misclassification to affected employees and their managers, even before the full audit is complete - accepting the disruption in exchange for avoiding the trust cost of delayed disclosure.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Complete the full audit first, then disclose only to employees whose specific development recommendations were materially affected.",s:1}]},
{id:"C1",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are facilitating a talent review with senior business leaders. The CEO endorses the succession recommendations quickly and enthusiastically. The business unit leaders stay largely silent. As the review ends you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Feel satisfied - CEO endorsement is the most important signal for a succession plan.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Note the dynamic and plan to follow up with the business unit leaders separately.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize that rapid CEO endorsement combined with business unit leader silence often signals compliance or unspoken concern about specific succession decisions - and before the review closes, create a specific moment for business unit leaders to raise concerns.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the CEO directly whether the business unit leaders share their view.",s:1}]},
{id:"C2",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"In a senior leadership team meeting, two executives disagree sharply about a people decision. One concedes within minutes and the meeting moves on. Afterward you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the agreed decision - the disagreement was resolved.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in with the executive who conceded to make sure they are comfortable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Note that the speed of concession is unlikely to reflect genuine alignment - and find a private moment to surface whether the unresolved tension will affect the people decision or the working relationship before it compounds.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the dynamic with the CHRO so they are aware.",s:1}]},
{id:"C3",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"A senior manager who has always been highly engaged with HR - proactively sharing team context, flagging people issues early - has become noticeably less forthcoming over the past three weeks. They still attend HR business partner meetings but contribute minimally. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Note no concern - they are still attending meetings.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in briefly to ask if everything is going well.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize the behavioral shift as a meaningful signal about something important changing in their world - find a private moment to genuinely understand what has changed, without presupposing what you will hear.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the change with the CHRO as a potential people risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"C4",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting people strategy options to the executive team. The HR data clearly supports one option. The COO is signaling through tone and body language - not words - that they have a strong preference for a different approach. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the data-supported recommendation regardless of the signal - the people data should drive the decision.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust your recommendation to leave room for the COO's implicit preference.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the data-supported recommendation clearly, then address the tension directly: 'I notice there may be a different operational perspective on this - I'd like to understand it before we finalize the people strategy.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Speak with the COO privately before the presentation to understand their preference and adjust the recommendation accordingly.",s:1}]},
{id:"C5",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You have been asked to assess the cultural readiness of an organization for a significant transformation. Senior HR and leadership present a confident narrative of strong cultural alignment. After two days of focus groups with employees, you sense significant anxiety, mistrust, and passive resistance beneath the official narrative. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Report on the official narrative - your brief was to assess readiness and leadership has confirmed alignment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Include a brief note that 'some employee concerns were raised.'",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present a candid cultural assessment that distinguishes the official narrative from the employee reality - naming the anxiety, mistrust, and resistance with specific evidence from your focus groups.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Share your findings with HR leadership verbally before writing the report to understand how much candor they want in the document.",s:1}]},
{id:"O1",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you approach AI assistance on significant HR analysis or L&D design work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I enter the people question or L&D brief into AI and work with what comes back. I refine if the first output is not useful.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a rough sense of what I need before prompting, but I rarely write this down or structure it explicitly.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before engaging AI on a significant HR or L&D problem, I write a clear problem statement - the people question, the organizational context, the decision it will inform - before writing the prompt.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I consistently produce a structured people context brief before AI engagement - the question, the population, the constraints, and what a useful output would enable the organization to do. I can show this to a colleague.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have made structured pre-prompt framing a team standard for HR and L&D work. My approach to AI problem definition has been adopted by professionals on my people team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O2",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you evaluate AI-generated HR analysis, learning designs, or talent frameworks:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"If the HR or L&D output looks comprehensive and addresses the prompt, I generally use it. I do not systematically evaluate against the underlying people question.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I sense when something is off with AI-generated HR or L&D output, but I cannot always articulate specifically what or why.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly evaluate AI-generated HR output against the original people question - not just the prompt - and I can explain my acceptance or rejection reasoning to a colleague.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My AI output evaluation for HR and L&D work is systematic. I can consistently identify the type of reasoning error in AI output and articulate specifically why I iterated or accepted.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed HR and L&D output evaluation frameworks for AI-assisted people work that my team now uses. My approach to AI quality has raised the standard of our people function deliverables.",s:5}]},
{id:"O3",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your AI iteration behavior when HR or L&D output is insufficient:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I typically accept the first or second output for people work. If it is not right I either use what is there or approach the problem differently.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I iterate when something is clearly wrong in the HR or L&D output but I do not have a systematic approach. I stop when the output feels adequate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I iterate with directed purpose on people work - each iteration is guided by a specific diagnosis of what the previous output missed about the human or organizational dimension.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My iteration discipline on HR and L&D work is high. I can distinguish between an iteration opportunity and a genuine people judgment call that requires HR expertise rather than further AI direction.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I coach junior HR and L&D professionals on AI iteration discipline. My team produces better AI-assisted people work because of how I have shaped the iteration practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"O4",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you design the role of AI in your HR or L&D workflow:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I use AI where it clearly helps in HR and L&D - job description drafts, survey analysis, learning content. I have not explicitly designed which people tasks AI should handle versus which require my professional judgment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a general sense of where AI adds value in HR and L&D work, but this is intuitive rather than deliberate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before significant people work, I explicitly consider what AI will generate, what HR or L&D judgment I will provide, and where professional people expertise is load-bearing - and I design accordingly.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I design Human-AI collaboration at the people function level. I make explicit decisions about which HR and L&D tasks AI handles and where qualified professional judgment is mandatory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I architect Human-AI division of HR and L&D work for my organization. The frameworks I have designed for AI-assisted people work governance are used beyond my immediate team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O5",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your reflective practice after AI-assisted HR or L&D work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"After AI-assisted people work I move directly to the next task. I do not typically reflect on the quality of my AI direction.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally note what worked or did not in an AI session for people work, but this is informal and inconsistent.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly reflect on the quality of my AI direction after significant HR or L&D engagements - what I prompted for, what I got, and what would have made my direction more precise.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My reflection practice on AI-assisted people work is documented. I maintain notes on AI direction decisions that inform how I approach similar problems in the future.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My reflection practice has produced HR and L&D team standards for AI-assisted people work that go beyond my own practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"T1",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you monitor trust in your key business partner relationships:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I am generally aware when business partner relationships feel collaborative or strained, but I do not actively monitor trust state.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I notice when trust shifts significantly in a business partner relationship - a change in information sharing, a withdrawal from collaboration - but I do not track trust systematically.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively monitor whether trust in my key business partner relationships is growing or declining, and I can describe the current trajectory of my three most important relationships.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I track trust state systematically in my business partner relationships. I can describe the specific moments and interactions that have shaped each key relationship's trust trajectory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I design trust architecture deliberately in my HR context. I have a clear strategy for each key business partner relationship and I adjust my engagement behavior proactively.",s:5}]},
{id:"T2",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle AI involvement in people decisions or HR deliverables:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I do not typically disclose which parts of my HR work were AI-assisted unless specifically asked.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I mention AI involvement if it seems relevant to the conversation, but I do not have a consistent approach.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am intentional about communicating AI involvement in people decisions and HR deliverables - framing it in a way that maintains confidence in the quality, fairness, and professional accountability of the work.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I proactively manage AI disclosure as a trust-building practice in HR - designing how and when to communicate AI involvement based on what I know about each stakeholder's expectations around people decisions.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed function-level frameworks for AI transparency in people work that others use. My approach to AI disclosure in HR has become a standard practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"T3",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle moments when trust in a business partner relationship is at risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to avoid situations where trust in business partner relationships might be damaged. I am more comfortable with smooth relationships than addressing friction directly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I address trust ruptures in business partner relationships when they become unavoidable, but I tend to wait until the damage is visible before responding.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I address business partner trust risks proactively - naming tensions before they become ruptures and taking deliberate action to repair relationships before damage compounds.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at trust recovery in high-stakes business partner situations. I can repair damaged relationships through deliberate, honest engagement even when the initial cause of damage was significant.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My trust recovery capability in business partner relationships is recognized. I am brought into relationship situations that other HR professionals have been unable to repair.",s:5}]},
{id:"T4",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your behavior under HR pressure or high-visibility people situations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Under significant HR pressure or sensitive people situations I sometimes behave in ways not fully consistent with how I would want to be seen.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am generally consistent, but high-stakes people situations occasionally reveal gaps between how I intend to behave and how I actually behave.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am reliably consistent across high-visibility and routine HR situations. My behavior in a sensitive termination conversation does not differ significantly from my behavior in a routine HR business partner meeting.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My consistency under HR pressure is recognized. Colleagues and business partners trust that I will handle sensitive people situations the same way regardless of who is watching.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My consistency under HR pressure is exceptional. I have been trusted with the most sensitive people situations specifically because my behavior is predictable and principled under stress.",s:5}]},
{id:"T5",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to make AI-assisted people decisions feel trustworthy to employees and business leaders:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I have not specifically thought about how AI involvement affects the trust employees or business leaders have in people decisions. I focus on the quality of the HR outcome.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am aware that some stakeholders are uncertain about AI in people decisions, but I do not have a specific approach to managing this.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively work to make AI-assisted people decisions feel transparent and trustworthy - explaining AI's role, naming its limitations, and making clear where professional HR judgment provides the accountability.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at introducing AI into people decision contexts where there is initial skepticism. I can shift stakeholder perception through deliberate communication and demonstrated fairness.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed organizational approaches to AI trust-building in people decisions that are used beyond my own work. My ability to make AI-assisted HR feel fair and credible is a recognized capability.",s:5}]},
{id:"R1",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you engage with domains outside HR, L&D, and people:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I primarily work within HR and L&D. I engage with other business functions when required but do not seek this out.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am curious about adjacent business functions and occasionally engage with commercial or operational topics, but this is informal and does not typically affect my HR work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly and deliberately engage with domains outside HR - commercial strategy, technology, finance - because I find the distance genuinely useful for making better people decisions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-functional engagement is a core part of how I practice HR and L&D. I have contributed meaningfully to commercial, operational, and strategic decisions, using the distance between people and other functions as a source of insight.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-functional Range is recognized. I am regularly brought into non-HR decisions because my outside people perspective generates insights that functional specialists cannot see.",s:5}]},
{id:"R2",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you use knowledge from outside HR in your people work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to apply HR and L&D thinking to HR and L&D problems. I do not typically look for cross-functional connections.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice parallels between HR and other functions, but I do not systematically use these observations to inform my people work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly transfer frameworks across functions - deliberately looking for how commercial, operational, or technology thinking applies to people questions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-functional mental model transfer is a characteristic of how I practice HR. I can point to specific instances where an insight from outside HR changed the quality of a people strategy recommendation.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to transfer frameworks from other functions into people work is recognized as a distinctive capability. I have produced people strategy insights through cross-functional synthesis that purely HR-trained professionals could not have generated.",s:5}]},
{id:"R3",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your recent contribution outside HR, L&D, and people:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My recent contributions have been primarily within HR and L&D. I have not made significant contributions to commercial, operational, or strategic decisions in the last three months.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have made minor contributions outside HR recently - attending commercial meetings, offering people opinions on operational decisions - but I would not describe these as substantive.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I have made at least one genuine contribution outside HR in the last three months - one where my involvement produced a meaningfully better business outcome.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I have made multiple substantive contributions outside HR in the last three months. The commercial, operational, or strategic stakeholders involved would recognize and describe the value I added.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"Contributing outside HR is a regular, significant part of my work. The contributions I make in commercial, operational, and strategic domains are recognized as among my most valuable professional contributions.",s:5}]},
{id:"R4",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to operate effectively in non-HR business contexts:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I find it difficult to contribute confidently in non-HR business contexts. I tend to defer to commercial or operational stakeholders even when I have relevant people perspective.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I can engage in non-HR business contexts, but I find it harder than people work and I am not always sure when my HR perspective adds genuine value versus creating complexity.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I can operate in non-HR business contexts by deliberately suspending people-first assumptions and approaching the domain with structured business curiosity.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at operating in non-HR business contexts. I know how to make my people expertise relevant to commercial, operational, or strategic problems without imposing HR frameworks where they do not apply.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to operate in non-HR business contexts is exceptional. I can become a genuine contributor to commercial, operational, or strategic decisions rapidly - bringing people perspective that business specialists cannot generate themselves.",s:5}]},
{id:"R5",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how non-HR experience affects your people work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My non-HR experiences are interesting but do not significantly affect how I approach people problems.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice that a commercial or operational experience has changed how I think about a people problem, but this is rare and unplanned.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Non-HR experiences regularly change how I approach people problems. I deliberately look for what commercial, operational, and technology contexts can teach me about human behavior in organizations.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"The influence runs both ways deliberately. I use HR thinking to illuminate commercial and operational problems, and I use commercial and operational experience to improve my people strategy.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-functional learning produces HR capabilities that purely people-function experience could not produce. This synthesis is recognized by colleagues as a genuine differentiator in how I approach complex people challenges.",s:5}]},
],
del:[
{id:"V1",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A client stakeholder says: 'We need a project plan for the AI implementation.' Before engaging AI to build it you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Begin building the project plan immediately - the request is clear enough to start.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Clarify the scope, timeline, and key milestones before starting.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Write a one-paragraph problem statement defining what decision this plan will inform, what 'implementation' means in this context, and what a useful plan would enable the client to do - before building a single workstream.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the client to provide a detailed requirements document before you engage.",s:1}]},
{id:"V2",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"AI produces a comprehensive project plan that answers the stated brief. Reviewing it, you notice the plan is technically correct but optimizes for speed when the client's real constraint is quality and regulatory compliance. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Deliver the plan - it answers the stated brief and meets the timeline requested.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Add a section noting that a quality and compliance focus might require timeline adjustment.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Rebuild the plan around quality and compliance as the primary constraints - a fast plan that fails compliance is worse than a slower one that passes.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present both plans - the speed-optimized version and the compliance-optimized version - and let the client decide which constraint to prioritize.",s:1}]},
{id:"V3",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"You are three hours into AI-assisted project risk analysis when you realize you have been assessing risks against the original scope - but the scope changed two weeks ago and the risk register was not updated. Starting over means losing the three hours. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Complete the analysis with the original scope - the risks are still broadly relevant.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Complete the analysis but add a note clarifying it was conducted against the original scope.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Start over with the current scope - risk analysis against the wrong scope will produce misleading conclusions for any mitigation decisions based on it.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Present the original scope analysis and a summary of scope changes, and let the steering committee decide whether the risk register needs rebuilding.",s:1}]},
{id:"V4",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"A junior project manager asks you to review their AI-assisted project status report before it goes to the client. The report is accurate but frames the project as on track when the underlying dependencies suggest a delay is highly likely within two weeks. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Approve it - the report is accurate as of today and the dependencies may resolve.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Flag the dependency risk as a comment and let the junior PM decide whether to include it.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Sit with them, reframe the status report to surface the dependency risk with appropriate confidence, and help them develop the habit of forward-looking honest reporting.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Rewrite the status report yourself to ensure the client has an accurate picture of delivery risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"V5",dim:"V",type:"sit",text:"Your delivery organization is about to invest significant effort in an AI-assisted project management system based on a delivery methodology designed for waterfall projects. You believe the methodology is fundamentally misaligned with the AI-augmented agile delivery your clients now expect - a sophisticated tool built on the wrong methodology will make the underlying problem worse. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Build the system as scoped - the methodology was agreed and reopening it creates delay and commercial risk.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise your concern informally with the delivery director.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Formally recommend revisiting the delivery methodology before building the system - accepting the delay this creates and taking accountability for the disruption.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Build the system as scoped but design it with configurable workflow parameters that allow agile adaptation over time.",s:1}]},
{id:"E1",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting AI-assisted project risk analysis to the programme steering committee in 45 minutes. The analysis is comprehensive but one risk severity rating feels significantly understated based on what you know about this client environment - you cannot immediately quantify the gap. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the risk analysis as modelled - it is comprehensive and the instinct may be wrong.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Downgrade the overall risk assessment to avoid escalating a concern you cannot fully justify.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Spend the 45 minutes specifically challenging the understated risk rating - then either revise it with supporting evidence or present it with an explicit flagging that your professional judgment suggests it may be materially understated.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Postpone the steering committee presentation until you can quantify the risk more precisely.",s:1}]},
{id:"E2",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"Your delivery team has been following an AI-recommended resource allocation plan for three weeks. Delivery velocity is below plan and team sentiment signals are deteriorating. The AI model continues to recommend maintaining the current allocation with high confidence. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the current allocation - three weeks is not enough to override a high-confidence resource plan.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Raise the concern with the programme director and defer to their judgment.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call to rebalance the team allocation based on your reading of velocity data and team dynamics - document your reasoning and take accountability for the change.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Commission a team health assessment before deciding whether to override the resource plan.",s:1}]},
{id:"E3",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are leading a delivery engagement and AI-assisted progress analysis contradicts what your most senior client stakeholder expects based on their operational experience of similar programmes. The model confidence is high. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the AI analysis as-is - high confidence is high confidence.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust the analysis presentation to reduce the contrast with the stakeholder's operational experience.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the analysis clearly, explain your delivery read of why it diverges from their experience, and invite the stakeholder to engage with the tension directly.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Reframe the analysis with different assumptions until the output aligns with the stakeholder's experience of similar programmes.",s:1}]},
{id:"E4",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"You are the most senior delivery professional available to make a critical programme decision - a go/no-go for a major release or a significant scope change. You have 60% of the information you would want. The decision window closes in three days. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Wait for the remaining information - critical delivery decisions require adequate data.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Escalate to the programme director so a more senior person takes accountability for the decision.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Make the call with 60% information, document your reasoning and the assumptions you are making, name the signals that would cause you to reverse it, and take full accountability.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Make a provisional decision and communicate it explicitly as subject to revision when the remaining information is available.",s:1}]},
{id:"E5",dim:"E",type:"sit",text:"An AI-assisted project reporting tool your team has used for two months has been producing materially inaccurate milestone completion data. Some of this data has been shared with the client in formal status reports. You do not yet know the full extent of the inaccuracy. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Quietly correct the tool and audit all reports internally before informing the client.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Inform your programme director and let senior delivery leadership decide how to handle client disclosure.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Immediately disclose the data inaccuracy to the client, even before the full audit is complete - accepting the relationship risk in exchange for avoiding the trust cost of delayed disclosure.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Complete the full audit first, then disclose only the specific reports that were materially inaccurate.",s:1}]},
{id:"C1",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are running a project steering committee. The client sponsor endorses every milestone and plan update quickly and enthusiastically. The client operations team stays largely silent. As the committee ends you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Feel satisfied - sponsor endorsement is the most important signal for a project steering committee.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Note the dynamic and plan to follow up with the operations team members separately.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize that rapid sponsor endorsement combined with operations team silence often signals compliance or unspoken operational concern - and before the committee closes, create a specific moment for operations team members to raise issues.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Ask the client sponsor directly whether the operations team shares their view.",s:1}]},
{id:"C2",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"In a project team meeting, two senior workstream leads disagree sharply about technical approach. One concedes within minutes and the meeting moves on. Afterward you:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Continue with the agreed approach - the disagreement was resolved.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in with the lead who conceded to make sure they are comfortable.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Note that the speed of concession is unlikely to reflect genuine alignment - and find a private moment to surface whether the unresolved technical disagreement will affect delivery quality before it becomes a problem.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Raise the dynamic with the programme director so they are aware.",s:1}]},
{id:"C3",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"A workstream lead who has been one of your most proactive delivery team members has become progressively quieter in project meetings over the past three weeks. They still complete their tasks but have stopped raising risks and contributing to problem-solving discussions. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Note no concern - they are still delivering.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Check in briefly to ask if they are okay.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Recognize the behavioral shift as a meaningful signal about something important changing for this person - find a private moment to genuinely understand what has changed, without presupposing what you will hear.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Flag the change in engagement to the programme director as a potential delivery risk.",s:1}]},
{id:"C4",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You are presenting project options to the client executive team. The delivery data clearly supports one approach. The client CTO is signaling through tone and body language - not words - that they have a strong preference for a technically different approach. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Present the delivery-supported recommendation regardless of the signal - the project data should drive the decision.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Adjust your recommendation to leave room for the CTO's implicit preference.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present the delivery-supported recommendation clearly, then address the tension directly: 'I notice there may be a technical perspective on this I haven't fully addressed - I'd like to understand it before we finalize.'",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Speak with the CTO privately before the presentation to understand their preference and adjust the recommendation accordingly.",s:1}]},
{id:"C5",dim:"C",type:"sit",text:"You have been asked to assess the delivery readiness of a client organization for a major technology programme. Senior leadership presents a confident narrative of organizational readiness. After two days of working sessions with the delivery teams, you sense significant concern about capacity, skill gaps, and competing priorities beneath the official narrative. You:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Report on the official leadership narrative - your brief was to assess readiness and leadership has confirmed it.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"Include a brief note that 'some capacity and capability concerns were identified.'",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Present a candid delivery readiness assessment that distinguishes the official narrative from the operational reality - naming the capacity, skill, and priority concerns with specific evidence.",s:4},{l:"D",t:"Share your findings with client leadership verbally before writing the report to understand how much candor they want in the delivery readiness assessment.",s:1}]},
{id:"O1",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you approach AI assistance on significant delivery planning or project analysis:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I enter the project problem or planning question into AI and work with what comes back. I refine if the first output is not useful.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a rough sense of what I need before prompting, but I rarely write this down or structure it explicitly.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before engaging AI on a significant delivery problem, I write a clear problem statement - the project context, the constraints, the decision it will inform - before writing the prompt.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I consistently produce a structured delivery context brief before AI engagement - the project scope, the constraints, the success criteria, and what a useful output would enable. I can show this to a colleague.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have made structured pre-prompt framing a team standard for delivery work. My approach to AI problem definition has been adopted by project managers on my delivery team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O2",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you evaluate AI-generated project plans, risk analyses, or delivery recommendations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"If the delivery output looks comprehensive and addresses the prompt, I generally use it. I do not systematically evaluate against the underlying project context.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I sense when something is off with AI-generated delivery analysis, but I cannot always articulate specifically what or why.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly evaluate AI-generated delivery output against the actual project context - not just the prompt - and I can explain my acceptance or rejection reasoning to a colleague.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My AI output evaluation for delivery work is systematic. I can consistently identify the type of reasoning error in AI delivery output and articulate specifically why I iterated or accepted.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed delivery output evaluation frameworks for AI-assisted project work that my team now uses. My approach to AI quality has raised the standard of our delivery analysis.",s:5}]},
{id:"O3",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your AI iteration behavior when delivery output is insufficient:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I typically accept the first or second output for project work. If it is not right I either use what is there or rebuild it manually.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I iterate when something is clearly wrong in the delivery output but I do not have a systematic approach. I stop when the output feels adequate for project purposes.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I iterate with directed purpose on delivery work - each iteration is guided by a specific diagnosis of what the previous output missed about the project context or constraints.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My iteration discipline on delivery work is high. I can distinguish between an iteration opportunity and a genuine delivery judgment call that requires professional project management expertise rather than further AI direction.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I coach junior project managers on AI iteration discipline. My delivery team produces better AI-assisted project analysis because of how I have shaped the iteration practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"O4",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you design the role of AI in your delivery workflow:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I use AI where it clearly helps in delivery - status reports, risk identification, resource modelling. I have not explicitly designed which project tasks AI should handle versus which require my professional delivery judgment.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have a general sense of where AI adds value in project delivery, but this is intuitive rather than deliberate.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Before significant delivery work, I explicitly consider what AI will generate, what project management judgment I will provide, and where professional delivery expertise is load-bearing - and I design accordingly.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I design Human-AI collaboration at the programme level. I make explicit decisions about which delivery tasks AI handles across the team and where qualified professional review is mandatory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I architect Human-AI division of delivery work for my organization. The frameworks I have designed for AI-assisted project management governance are used beyond my immediate team.",s:5}]},
{id:"O5",dim:"O",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your reflective practice after AI-assisted delivery work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"After AI-assisted project work I move directly to the next task. I do not typically reflect on the quality of my AI direction.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally note what worked or did not in an AI session for delivery work, but this is informal and inconsistent.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly reflect on the quality of my AI direction after significant delivery engagements - what I prompted for, what I got, and what would have made my direction more precise.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My reflection practice on AI-assisted delivery work is documented. I maintain notes on AI direction decisions that inform how I approach similar project challenges in the future.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My reflection practice has produced delivery team standards for AI-assisted project management that go beyond my own work.",s:5}]},
{id:"T1",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you monitor trust in your key client and stakeholder relationships:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I am generally aware when client relationships feel collaborative or strained, but I do not actively monitor trust state.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I notice when trust shifts significantly in a client relationship - a change in meeting tone, a withdrawal from collaborative problem-solving - but I do not track trust systematically.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively monitor whether trust in my key client and stakeholder relationships is growing or declining, and I can describe the trajectory of my three most important relationships.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I track client trust state systematically. I can describe the specific moments and interactions that have shaped each key client relationship's trust trajectory.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I design client trust architecture deliberately in my delivery context. I have a clear strategy for each key stakeholder relationship and I adjust my engagement behavior proactively.",s:5}]},
{id:"T2",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle AI involvement in project reports or delivery analysis presented to clients:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I do not typically disclose which parts of my project reports or delivery analysis were AI-assisted unless specifically asked.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I mention AI involvement if it seems relevant to the client conversation, but I do not have a consistent approach.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am intentional about communicating AI involvement in project deliverables - framing it in a way that maintains client confidence in the quality, accuracy, and professional accountability of the delivery work.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I proactively manage AI disclosure as a trust-building practice with clients - designing how and when to communicate AI involvement based on what I know about each client's expectations around delivery rigour.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed team-level frameworks for AI transparency in project delivery that others use. My approach to AI disclosure in delivery work has become a standard practice.",s:5}]},
{id:"T3",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you handle moments when trust in a client delivery relationship is at risk:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to avoid situations where client trust in delivery might be damaged. I am more comfortable with smooth client relationships than addressing delivery friction directly.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I address client trust ruptures in delivery contexts when they become unavoidable, but I tend to wait until the damage is visible before responding.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I address client trust risks proactively in delivery - naming tensions before they become ruptures and taking deliberate action to repair relationships before damage compounds programme outcomes.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at trust recovery in high-stakes delivery situations. I can repair damaged client relationships through deliberate, honest engagement even when the initial cause of damage was a significant delivery failure.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My trust recovery capability in delivery relationships is recognized. I am brought into client relationship situations that other delivery professionals have been unable to repair.",s:5}]},
{id:"T4",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your behavior under delivery pressure or high-visibility programme situations:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Under significant delivery pressure or escalated client situations I sometimes behave in ways not fully consistent with how I would want to be seen.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am generally consistent, but high-stakes delivery situations occasionally reveal gaps between how I intend to behave and how I actually behave.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I am reliably consistent across high-visibility and routine delivery situations. My behavior in a delivery crisis does not differ significantly from my behavior in a routine project review.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"My consistency under delivery pressure is recognized. Clients and colleagues trust that I will manage a delivery crisis with the same professionalism as a routine project update.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My consistency under delivery pressure is exceptional. I have been trusted with the most critical delivery situations specifically because my behavior is predictable and principled under stress.",s:5}]},
{id:"T5",dim:"T",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to make AI-assisted delivery feel trustworthy to clients:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I have not specifically thought about how AI involvement affects the trust clients have in my delivery work. I focus on the quality of project outcomes.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am aware that some clients are uncertain about AI in project management, but I do not have a specific approach to managing this.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I actively work to make AI-assisted delivery feel transparent and trustworthy - explaining AI's role, naming its limitations, and making clear where professional delivery judgment provides the accountability.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at introducing AI into delivery relationships where there is initial skepticism. I can shift client perception through deliberate communication and demonstrated delivery quality.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"I have developed organizational approaches to AI trust-building in delivery contexts that are used beyond my own programmes. My ability to make AI-assisted delivery feel credible is a recognized capability.",s:5}]},
{id:"R1",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you engage with domains outside delivery and project management:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I primarily work within delivery and project management. I engage with other business functions when required but do not seek this out.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I am curious about adjacent domains and occasionally engage with commercial or technical topics, but this is informal and does not typically affect my delivery work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly and deliberately engage with domains outside delivery - commercial strategy, technology architecture, people dynamics - because I find the distance genuinely useful for making better programme decisions.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-domain engagement is a core part of how I deliver programmes. I have contributed meaningfully to commercial, technical, and people decisions, using the distance between delivery and other domains as a source of programme insight.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-domain Range is recognized. I am regularly brought into non-delivery decisions because my outside programme perspective generates insights that functional specialists cannot see.",s:5}]},
{id:"R2",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how you use knowledge from outside delivery in your project work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I tend to apply project management thinking to project management problems. I do not typically look for cross-domain connections.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice parallels between delivery and other domains, but I do not systematically use these observations to inform my project work.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I regularly transfer frameworks across domains - deliberately looking for how commercial, technical, or people thinking applies to delivery challenges.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"Cross-domain mental model transfer is a characteristic of how I deliver programmes. I can point to specific instances where an insight from outside delivery changed how I managed a critical programme challenge.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to transfer frameworks from other domains into delivery is recognized as a distinctive capability. I have solved delivery challenges through cross-domain synthesis that purely delivery-trained professionals could not have resolved.",s:5}]},
{id:"R3",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your recent contribution outside delivery and project management:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My recent contributions have been primarily within delivery and project management. I have not made significant contributions to commercial, technical, or people decisions in the last three months.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I have made minor contributions outside delivery recently - attending commercial or technical meetings, offering delivery opinions on strategic decisions - but I would not describe these as substantive.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I have made at least one genuine contribution outside delivery in the last three months - one where my involvement produced a meaningfully better business outcome.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I have made multiple substantive contributions outside delivery in the last three months. The commercial, technical, or people stakeholders involved would recognize and describe the value I added.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"Contributing outside delivery is a regular, significant part of my work. The contributions I make in commercial, technical, and people domains are recognized as among my most valuable professional contributions.",s:5}]},
{id:"R4",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes your ability to operate effectively in non-delivery business contexts:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"I find it difficult to contribute confidently in non-delivery business contexts. I tend to defer to commercial or technical stakeholders even when I have relevant delivery perspective.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I can engage in non-delivery business contexts, but I find it harder than project work and I am not always sure when my delivery perspective adds genuine value versus creating noise.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"I can operate in non-delivery business contexts by deliberately suspending project management assumptions and approaching the domain with structured business curiosity.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"I am skilled at operating in non-delivery business contexts. I know how to make my project management expertise relevant to commercial, technical, or people problems without imposing delivery frameworks where they do not apply.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My ability to operate in non-delivery business contexts is exceptional. I can become a genuine contributor to commercial, technical, or people decisions rapidly - bringing structured delivery perspective that domain specialists cannot generate themselves.",s:5}]},
{id:"R5",dim:"R",type:"beh",text:"Select the statement that most accurately describes how non-delivery experience affects your project work:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"My non-delivery experiences are interesting but do not significantly affect how I approach project management challenges.",s:1},{l:"B",t:"I occasionally notice that a commercial or technical experience has changed how I think about a delivery challenge, but this is rare and unplanned.",s:2},{l:"C",t:"Non-delivery experiences regularly change how I approach project challenges. I deliberately look for what commercial, technical, and people contexts can teach me about effective programme delivery.",s:3},{l:"D",t:"The influence runs both ways deliberately. I use delivery thinking to illuminate commercial and technical problems, and I use commercial and technical experience to improve my programme management.",s:4},{l:"E",t:"My cross-domain learning produces delivery capabilities that purely project management experience could not produce. This synthesis is recognized by colleagues as a genuine differentiator in how I manage complex programmes.",s:5}]},
],
};

module.exports = QB;
